"[2611.00081] Two-adic towers of exceptions for sums of four numbers x(ax+b), and a conjecture of Sun | aiXiv (https://aixiv.online/abs/2611.00081)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 days ago; Crawled: 2 days ago; Mathematics > Number Theory ... Sun conjectured (arXiv:2411.14308, Conjecture 5.1) that for integers a b 0 with b even, gcd(a,b) = 1 and (a,b) (3,2), infinitely many positive multiples of 4 are not of the form w(aw+b)+x(ax+b)+y(ay+b)+z(az+b) with integers w, x, y, z. ... The special case b = a 1 (sums of four generalized m-gonal numbers with m 0 mod 4, m 8) was proved earlier by D.\n\nMathematics > Number Theory\n\n[Published 2026-11-02 on zenodo.org; indexed on aiXiv 2 Nov 2026]\n\n# Two-adic towers of exceptions for sums of four numbers x(ax+b), and a conjecture of Sun\n\nAaron Cooper\n\npreprint Claude, ChatGPT AI-assisted\n\nView PDFDiscuss (0)Reproduce\n\n> Abstract: Z.-W. Sun conjectured (arXiv:2411.14308, Conjecture 5.1) that for integers a b 0 with b even, gcd(a,b) = 1 and (a,b) (3,2), infinitely many positive multiples of 4 are not of the form w(aw+b)+x(ax+b)+y(ay+b)+z(az+b) with integers w, x, y, z. We give a short elementary proof, valid for every odd a 5 and every even b prime to a. The reason is 2-adic: a sum of four squares divisible by 8 has only even terms, and each forced halving moves the residue class of the terms modulo a. The special case b = a 1 (sums of four generalized m-gonal numbers with m 0 mod 4, m 8) was proved earlier by D. Kim (arXiv:2006.04490) with the same descent. Combined with two theorems of Sun, the descent shows that the exceptional set is always a finite union of explicit \"towers\" plus finitely many values, and we determine the exceptional sets for (a,b) = (5,2) and (5,4) completely, answering Sun's Remark 1.5. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2611.00062] On a conjecture of Sun on sums of four numbers x(5x±1)/2 | aiXiv (https://aixiv.online/abs/2611.00062)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 days ago; Crawled: 2 days ago; # On a conjecture of Sun on sums of four numbers x(5x±1)/2 ... Sun conjectured (arXiv:2411.14308, Conjecture 5.5) that every integer n 51 can be written as w(5w 1)/2 + x(5x 1)/2 + y(5y+1)/2 + z(5z+1)/2 with w, x, y, z natural numbers. ... The mathematics, code and audits were produced by the AI system Claude (Anthropic) under the author's direction; see Section 8 of the paper.\n\nMathematics > Number Theory\n\n[Published 2026-11-02 on zenodo.org; indexed on aiXiv 2 Nov 2026]\n\n# On a conjecture of Sun on sums of four numbers x(5x±1)/2\n\nAaron Cooper\n\npreprint Claude, ChatGPT AI-assisted\n\nThe AI labels come from the paper's own statement. Authors: claim the paper to set them, or write to [email protected].\n\nView PDFDiscuss (0)Reproduce\n\n> Abstract: Z.-W. Sun conjectured (arXiv:2411.14308, Conjecture 5.5) that every integer n 51 can be written as w(5w 1)/2 + x(5x 1)/2 + y(5y+1)/2 + z(5z+1)/2 with w, x, y, z natural numbers. We prove the stronger statement that every natural number n except n = 1 and n = 19 has such a representation. The problem reduces to finding lattice points on a sphere in a coset of 10Z⁴ with exactly two negative coordinates. We detect these with a degree-8 polynomial weight, and bound the resulting theta series of weights 2 to 10 and level 400 through explicit newform decompositions and Deligne's bound. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA four-term representation by pentagonal quadratic polynomials | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/365897/a-four-term-representation-by-pentagonal-quadratic-polynomia)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Four-term pentagonal representation conjecture. ... Zhi-Wei Sun, “New results similar to Lagrange's four-square theorem”, arXiv:2411.14308 (2024).\n\nA four-term representation by pentagonal quadratic polynomials\n\nLet $\\mathbb N$N denote the nonnegative integers. Four-term pentagonal representation conjecture. Every integer $n>51$n>51 can be written as\n\n$$n=\\frac{w(5w-1)}2+\\frac{x(5x-1)}2+\\frac{y(5y+1)}2+\\frac{z(5z+1)}2$$n=2 w(5 w−1)​+2 x(5 x−1)​+2 y(5 y+1)​+2 z(5 z+1)​\n\nwith $w,x,y,z\\in\\mathbb N$w,x,y,z∈N.\n\nThis is an explicit eventual universality claim analogous to Lagrange’s four-square theorem, with nonnegative variables and two signs in the linear terms. No resolution is stated in the source.\n\n## Primary source\n\nZhi-Wei Sun, “New results similar to Lagrange's four-square theorem”, arXiv:2411.14308 (2024).\n\nadditive-number-theorypolygonal-numbers\n\n0 0\n\n3 weeks ago\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\n[Button: Solve with an agent]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMirror invariance conjecture for the Thue–Morse two-block language | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/353752/mirror-invariance-conjecture-for-the-thue-morse-two-block-la)\nciteturn1search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; # Mirror invariance conjecture for the Thue–Morse two-block language ... Michel Dekking and Mike Keane, “Two-block substitutions and morphic words”, arXiv:2202.13548 (2023).\n\n# Mirror invariance conjecture for the Thue–Morse two-block language\n\nAbout 4 years old · traced to 2022\n\nLet $x^{(00)}$x(00) be the fixed point of the Thue–Morse two-block substitution, and let $L_{\\rm\\sc TM}$L\\sc TM​ be the language of all finite words occurring in $x^{(00)}$x(00). A set of binary words is mirror invariant if, whenever it contains $w_1\\dots w_m$w 1​…w m​, it also contains $(1-w_1)\\dots(1-w_m)$(1−w 1​)…(1−w m​). Mirror invariance conjecture. The language $L_{\\rm\\sc TM}$L\\sc TM​ is mirror invariant. This is contrasted with the fact that the language is not reversal invariant; proving mirror invariance is difficult because mirror images of occurring words may first appear only after many substitution iterations.\n\nReferences\n\n## Primary source\n\nMichel Dekking and Mike Keane, “Two-block substitutions and morphic words”, arXiv:2202.13548 (2023).\n\nmorphic-wordssymbolic-dynamics\n\n0 0\n\nPosted 1 month ago\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\n[Button: Solve with an agent]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTwo-block substitutions and morphic word... (https://axi.lims.ac.uk/paper/2202.13548)\nciteturn1search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.7 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; ID: 2202.13548 ID: 2202.13548\n\nID: 2202.13548 ID: 2202.13548\n\nSearch\n\n# Two-block substitutions and morphic words\n\nFebruary 28, 2022\n\nView on ArXiv\n\nMichel Dekking, Mike Keane\n\nMathematics\n\nCombinatorics\n\nWe consider in general two-block substitutions and their fixed points. We prove that some of them have a simple structure: their fixed points are morphic sequences. Others are intrinsically more complex, such as the Kolakoski sequence. We prove this for the Thue-Morse sequence in base 3/2.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Thue–Morse two-block fixed point has frequency one half | trureturing (https://www.trureturing.org/research/dekking-keane-2022-two-block-thue-morse-frequency/)\nciteturn1search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Resolution source: D5/S1/Words/TwoBlockSubstitution/ThueMorseFrequency.result 10.48550/arXiv.2202.13548 Source dossier Release 36a49830636b ... The v1 paper states Conjecture 4 and OEIS A354896 records the density as an unsolved problem.\n\n# The Thue–Morse two-block fixed point has frequency one half\n\nLiterature status: not rechecked Repository record: proved\n\nResolution source: D5/S1/Words/TwoBlockSubstitution/ThueMorseFrequency.result10.48550/arXiv.2202.13548Source dossierRelease 36a49830636b\n\nProblem\n\n## The question\n\nDekking and Keane, Two-block substitutions and morphic words, arXiv:2202.13548v1, Section 4, define $\\kappa_{\\rm TM}(00)=001$, $\\kappa_{\\rm TM}(01)=010$, $\\kappa_{\\rm TM}(10)=101$, and $\\kappa_{\\rm TM}(11)=110$. Their fixed point $x^{(00)}$ starts with $00$ and satisfies $x_{3n}=x_{2n}$, $x_{3n+1}=x_{2n+1}$, and $x_{3n+2}=1-x_{2n+1}$.\n\n> Conjecture 4. The frequency of 1 in $x^{(00)}$ exists and equals $\\frac12$.\n\nThe source is v1, Section 4, pp. 5–6. ArXiv v2 and the journal version change the substitution; this conclusion concerns the displayed v1 table.\n\n## Our foothold\n\nThe assertion asks for the limit over every natural prefix length, rather than a numerical approximation at selected lengths. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA377224 - OEIS (https://oeis.org/A377224/internal)\nciteturn1search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.0 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; `%C Conjecture 1: a(n) = 0 only for n = 1. ... `%H Zhi-Wei Sun, <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.14308\">New results similar to Lagrange's four-square theorem</a>, arXiv:2411.14308 [math.NT], 2024.\n\n`%C {x*(5*x+1)/2 + y*(5*y+1)/2 + z*(5*z+1)/2: x,y,z are integers} = N\\{1,10,19,94}. `\n\n`%C Conjecture 3: We have `\n\n`%C {x*(5*x+3) + y*(5*y+3)/2 + 3*z*(5*z+3)/2: x,y,z are integers} = N\\{31,77}, `\n\n`%C {x*(5*x+3) + y*(5*y+3)/2 + 5*z*(5*z+3): x,y,z are integers} = N\\{10,16}, `\n\n`%C and `\n\n`%C {x*(5*x+3)/2 + y*(5*y+3)/2 + 5*z*(5*z+3)/2: x,y,z are integers} = N\\{3,15,29,44}. `\n\n`%H Zhi-Wei Sun, <a href=\"/A377224/b377224.txt\">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..10000</a> `\n\n`%H Zhi-Wei Sun, <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jnt.2